#[
  #set text(size: 9.0pt)
  #set par(leading: 0.63em, spacing: 0.68em)

  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Scarcity and allocation — the definition turns on scarce resources and competing wants, not on money, trade or bookkeeping.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Unlimited wants against limited resources. The reverse pairing, equal abundance of both, is the answer that reads quickly and is wrong.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    What, how and for whom — the allocation questions. Price is the answer in a market economy, not one of the questions.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The next best alternative forgone. Money paid is the money cost, and the sum of every alternative is a figure nobody could give up.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    4 × 3,000 = ₦12,000 in wages forgone. ₦3,000 is one hour's pay, and zero confuses no cash payment with no cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Abundance, not charity. A cheap good is still an economic good because it is scarce relative to demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The permanent gap between wants and resources. A shortage is a market condition; scarcity is the reason markets exist.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rice is scarce relative to demand and carries a price. The other three are free goods in ordinary circumstances.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The maximum attainable combinations. Each distractor describes another curve: demand, marginal cost and the consumption function.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    On the curve is efficiency. Unemployment lies inside and an unattainable combination lies outside.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Inside the curve the economy could produce more of either good without giving anything up, which is the signature of idle resources.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Outside is unattainable with present resources and technology; growth or better technology moves the curve out and makes it reachable.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Increasing opportunity cost. A straight line would mean every resource was equally good at producing both goods.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Better technology raises capacity. Unemployment and reallocation move the point, not the curve, and a price change does not move either.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Land, labour, capital and enterprise. Money and raw materials are not factors: money buys capital, and raw materials are part of land.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rent. Interest belongs to capital, profit to the entrepreneur and wages to labour.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Profit, and it is a residual: the entrepreneur is paid last and only if anything is left.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Capital, the produced means of production. The four rewards are a fixed pairing and the exam tests the pairing directly.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Capital produces; money only buys. The test is whether the thing can be used to make other goods.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sewing machine is used to make other goods. The cement, bread and note are consumed, sold or held, not used to produce.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The price mechanism. Planning and decrees describe a command economy, and custom describes a traditional one.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A central authority decides what, how and for whom. Prices may exist, but they do not allocate.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Mixed: private ownership and prices operate alongside state ownership, taxation and regulation.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Education and vaccination are the examples: the state subsidises or compels them because the social benefit exceeds the private choice.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Street lighting and defence: one person's use does not reduce another's, and no one can easily be excluded — which is why the market under-provides them.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The next best use of the same resources. The money is a transfer, not a cost in the economist's sense.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Choice is the consequence of scarcity. Expensiveness and poverty are separate matters, and production below capacity is unemployment.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Micro studies the individual unit and the market; macro studies the economy as a whole. The other three are topics inside the two.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ought is the marker of a value judgement. The other three are positive statements that can be checked against data.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Positive statements describe and can be tested; normative statements judge and cannot.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    100 ÷ 50 = 2 tonnes of rice per tonne of beans. The inverse, half a tonne, is the cost of one tonne of rice.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Reallocation with capacity unchanged. Growth is the curve shifting out, and unemployment is a point moving inside.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Scarcity is universal, which is why every system, rich or poor, must choose.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Time spent studying is income forgone. The other three are money costs or transfers, not forgone alternatives.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Wages or salary. Labour is human effort, mental or physical, and its reward is the payment for that effort.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Traditional. The answers are inherited rather than chosen, which is the fourth system some syllabuses name.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Efficiency is the on-the-curve condition. Employing everybody is not the same as employing them well, which is why a point inside the curve can still have full employment.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    To get more of one good you must give up some of the other, which is opportunity cost drawn as a slope.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Land rent and capital interest. The other three swap the rewards, which is the most common trap in the topic.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Capital goods raise future capacity, which shifts the PPC outward. The sacrifice of current consumer goods is the opportunity cost of that growth.
  ]

]

